    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220">

                <pb facs="0336" n="289" xml:id="pg289"/>

                <head>220. Telegram 1624 to London<anchor n="1" xml:id="d220-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1955-09-22">September 22, 1955, 7:59
                            p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d220-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d220-1"/>
                <p>1624. Your 1119. Department considers it mere Chinese Communist quibble whether
                    GOI and UK should be “invited”, “notified” or “requested” to perform allotted
                    functions. Department naturally communicated with both GOI and UKG in regard
                    U.S. interest in their functions. It assumed PRC would do likewise.</p>
                <p>PRC clearly obligated make arrangements necessary to enable UK Government
                    function. U.S. Government promptly discharged its obligation to enable GOI to
                    function.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Hoover</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d220-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d220-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 611.93/9–2055. Confidential. Drafted
                    by McConaughy; cleared by Sebald and Robertson. Repeated to Geneva for Johnson
                    as telegram 772.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
